import FV.Link.ExeLdIn

/-! # Non-vacuity of `Link.DeadCleanup.compileExe_total_ldIn` on real link inputs (A1, #409)

`lean-ld`'s inputs are two ELF objects as the toolchain writes them (bytes below, made by
`clang --target=aarch64-linux-musl` and, for the region, `cargo fv`'s program-part steps:
`rust-lld -flavor gnu -r`, `llvm-objcopy --rename-section .text=.text.fvlean` and
`--add-symbol=__fvlean_start=.text.fvlean:0,global`):

* `start.o`: `_start` calls `add1` (`R_AARCH64_CALL26`) and exits; `.data` holds `add1`'s
  address (`R_AARCH64_ABS64`);
* `add1.fvlean.rcgu.o`: the region `.text.fvlean` (256 bytes) holding the function `add1`.

The link spec's program part is the CLIF function `add1` (the spill-fallback allocation), its
addresses left to `lean-ld`'s placement (`Link.DeadCleanup.ldSpec`). Every premise of
`compileExe_total_ldIn` is decided on these inputs (`premises`, `native_decide`), so `lean-ld`
links them and the shipped compiler succeeds on the executable (`compileExe_total_ldIn_witness`).
(Real `cargo fv` executables link `std`: tens of MB of input objects, measured by `lean-link --ld`
instead, docs/research/lean-linker.md.)
-/

namespace Link.DeadCleanup.LdInWitness

open Link Link.Ld E2E E2E.LinkCheck

